cdk: define the default arrow on cursorless grabs over foreign cursors

The cursorless grabs taken while the pointer shows one of our own
cursors, or sits on the frame of the grabbed toplevel, already define
the theme's default arrow on the grab window: a keyboard-driven grab
must not pin an application or frame cursor over the whole screen.  A
grab taken while the pointer shows a cursor none of our windows
provided (the root, or a window of another client) still freezes that
foreign image on the grab window instead, so that the toggling grabs of
a held key do not flicker the cursor between the arrow and the window
under the pointer at the key repeat rate while the pointer sits still.

But the frozen image pins the foreign cursor over the whole screen for
as long as the grab lasts, and a menu opened with a single F10 press
with the pointer parked on another application's text box then keeps
the text cursor on the screen while the menu is open, promising a click
the menu is going to eat, the first click only dismisses the menu.
Define the theme's default arrow there as well, the grab must not pin a
foreign cursor either, and the arrow matches what a click does under
the grab; the churn of a held key flickers the cursor between the arrow
and the window under the pointer while the key repeats, freezing the
image avoided that flicker, but the steady foreign cursor is precisely
the promise the grab breaks.

The helpers that classified the cursor the pointer was showing for the
grab are not needed anymore and go away.  A theme that fails to provide
the default arrow falls back to freezing the exact image the pointer
shows, the visible cursor does not change at all and the stale frame
cursor does not come back.
